\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia: Suharto Urges Banks To Deal With Bad Loans \b0\par\par\uc2 176 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 1997\par 08:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--President Suharto urged Indonesian banks Thursday to overcome a growing problem of bad loans. \par \par \uc2  'I want to draw your attention to the issue of bad debt, although progress has been made in general to cope with the problem,' he said when opening a conference of bank executives. \par \par \uc2  According to Bank Indonesia, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bad loans amounted to 10.2 trillion rupiahs ($3.9 billion) or 2.93% of bank credits in April 1997. The percentage rose from 2.58% in December 1996 after a sharp fall from 3.29% in December 1995. \par \par \uc2  State-owned banks recorded 6.7 trillion rupiah ($2.6 billion) in bad loans. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono said the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now on an improving trend after a 5.4% drop against the U.S. dollar earlier this week. \par \par \uc2  Djiwandono said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  has headed off speculative attacks that had weakened the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 24-07-97 \par \par \uc2  0654GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt7o05f87\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
