\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia Widens  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2 's Trading Band After Manila's Move \b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 1997\par 15:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA -(Dow Jones)- Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday widened its dollar-rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band to 12% from 8%, creating even greater flexibility to its exchange-rate mechanism. \par \par \uc2  The head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Department of Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Djakaria, said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band is now set 2,374 rupiah to 2,678 rupiah to the dollar. Prior to the widening, the band was at 2,430 rupiah to 2,622 rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia's move followed the Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move earlier Friday to widen its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading band. The Philippine peso has been under attack from \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators in recent weeks, following a move by the Bank of Thailand to float the baht July 2. \par \par \uc2  Unlike the Philippine government's move, however - which caused the peso to fall over 10% in trading Friday - the Indonesian rupiah lost only about 0.5% in volatile trading, ending trading in Asia at 2,445.50 rupiah to the dollar, down from 2,432.75 rupiah Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has been virtually free from speculative attacks over the last two months, while neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Thailand, the Philippines, and Malaysia have been battered. All three countries have been forced to shell out billions of dollars defending their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Bank Indonesia has been able to stay comfortably on the sidelines. \par \par \uc2  The fact that Bank Indonesia took the prudent steps last year to widen the rupiah's trading band to 8% from 3% had been a major reason for the rupiah's stability. By removing itself from regulating the rupiah's value, Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  effectively transferred the risk to the market. \par \par \uc2  Market players said the bank's move was pre-emptive, and that by widening the rupiah's trading band to virtually a free float, offshore speculators will find it harder to galvanize a concerted attack against the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "Bank Indonesia made a very smart, prudent policy move by deciding to widen and caught most in the market off guard," Daniel Lian, the head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed-income research at NatWest Markets in Singapore, said. "With over a 300 (rupiah) band to deal with, I don't know how offshore players could effectively launch a cohesive attack." \par \par \uc2  Traders pointed to Indonesia's stronger economic fundamentals - as well as its already flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band - as the reason for the rupiah's strength to date. Friday's move continued a liberalization of Indonesia's exchange-rate mechanism that economists had been hoping for. \par \par \uc2  "Pressure was building up against the rupiah, though many see Indonesia as a much different story than Thailand," Lian said. "But with the Philippines and Thailand out of the way, it wasn't impossible speculators would focus on Indonesia." \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts noted that in a tightly regulated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system - such as those previously held in Thailand and the Philippines - speculators can easily coordinate attacks against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they know exactly the level to go in at and where \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support will be. A wider band, however, offers that much more uncertainty for any speculator, and that much more risk. \par \par \uc2  "It is nearly impossible to speculate with a 12% band, as traders have to drive down the value that much more to see profits," said Neil Saker, Singapore-based economic research head at Socgen-Crosby Securities. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dt7b00i4i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
