\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesian rupiah rises in late trading, swap down. \b0\par\par\uc2 456 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 1996\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1996 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Sept 18 (Reuter) - The Indonesian rupiah rose further against the dollar in active Wednesday trading on capital inflow from offshore investment houses, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  One major local bank dealer said the dollar was well supported at the 2,320.0 level at midday before that became the resistance level later on European selling. \par \par \uc2  He said Singapore operators were seen taking profit at that level but then unloaded dollars as European markets opened. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said it seemed to have been a repeat of the past two days when the rupiah appreciated early in the afternoon as London and other European markets opened. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there was market talk of an inflow of fresh funds from European investment houses, mainly from London. \par \par \uc2  Spot rupiah was quoted at 2,318.0/19.0 at 0900 GMT against an opening of 2,321.0/22.00. \par \par \uc2  "Sentiment on the rupiah remains firm. Operators were also seen unloading swaps," the local bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Swap premiums fell as offshore operators sold forwards. \par \par \uc2  "They could not park all the money into rupiah-denominated papers, so they sold forwards for now," he said. \par \par \uc2  Tomorrow and today rupiah closed at 2,317.90/18.35 and 2,318.75/19.25, respectively. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah has risen steadily against the dollar since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widened its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band last week to eight percent from five percent. \par \par \uc2  Some economists were worried over the possible implication for Indonesian exports of a strong rupiah against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  But a UBS report said it was not necessarily so as oil exports were not rupiah-based and traditional non-oil exports were suffering from non-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes such as labour costs. \par \par \uc2  "Indeed, a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would facilitate imports of capital goods and raw materials, necessary to support capacity expansion in emerging, higher value-added export areas." \par \par \uc2  "So far, however, there is no evidence of any shift from a stable real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy." \par \par \uc2  One economist said a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also reduce imported inflation which was present due to rising investments. \par \par \uc2  Overnight swap was at 0.40/0.45 and tom/next at 0.35/0.45. \par \par \uc2  One-month swap fell to 14.30/15.00 from 15.25/16.25, two to 30.0/31.5 from 30.50/32.50, three to 45.5/46.5 from 47.00/48.005 and six to 94.5/95.5 from 96.00/97.00 points. \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by one point to 2,311/2,503 and the conversion rate also by one point to 2,311/2,359 on Wednesday. -- Jakarta newsroom +6221 384-6364. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1996 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018ds9i02yu1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
