\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia's Economic Moves Seen Prudent, Says IMF \b0\par\par\uc2 129 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 1996\par 11:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move to widen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  band to 8% from 5% is considered prudent as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  removes itself from micromanaging the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the International Monetary Fund's Indonesia representative said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bank said Wednesday it was widening the band. \par \par \uc2  Kadhim Al-Eyd also noted that the country's diminishing trade surplus is seen as manageable as it looks to be primarily a cyclical problem. \par \par \uc2  With regard to its trade record, Indonesia benefitts from having strong fundamental economic policies already in place, Al-Eyd said. \par \par \uc2  Al-Eyd was speaking at a press conference in Jakarta to release the IMF's annual report. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 12-09-96 \par \par \uc2  0935GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds9c07ein\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
